            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is already quite air tight and will become a little tighter based on the items we talked about:



** The chimney for the current boiler &amp; the 4" combustion air supply pipe will be eliminated when you upgrade the boiler



** The bathroom exhaust fan on the main floor has a bit of leakage through it &amp; would benefit from an upgrade to a new Energy Star rated unit that has a good butterfly damper to prevent air leakage heat loss when the fan is not in operation



Savings: I would estimate your house will become about 10% more air tight when the above upgrades are implemented, which will need to be offset by an increased need for adequate ventilation. Please see the Ventilation Sections of this report.



There are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x4 (R12). It appears the "wood siding" section of these walls has been previously retrofitted with an additional 1.5" (R7.5) of extruded polystyrene insulation. The section of walls with the brick exterior will not have this additional exterior insulation so is the original R12.



We talked about the possible benefit of increasing all of the exterior walls up to a minimum R32. This would mean adding another R12.5 (perhaps using another 2.5" of extruded polystyrene) to the wood exterior walls and adding R20 (ie: 4" of XTPS) to the brick exterior walls. This would be a large &amp; expensive job, so having me estimate the cost savings will help you evaluate if you really want to do this.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). These rebates are pro-rated based on the amount of wall area that is upgraded. In your case, the brick walls represent 18% of the main floor perimeter and the wood siding walls represent the other 82%.



Savings:

By increasing the wood siding walls (82%) by R12.5 and the brick exterior walls (18%) by R20, you could potentially save $ 374 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,900 (18% x $8,000 + 82% x $3,000) in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic hatch inside the house was sealed shut, however you were able to confirm from a Home Inspection Report) there is about R32 of insulation up there, and some of the insulation is confirmed to be vermiculite. From using my Infrared Camera, I could see that the attic insulation is even throughout the house and no significant "cold spots" noted.



At R32, you are likely OK not adding anymore. As an FYI - I did model the energy savings associated with an increase to R50.



NOTE: Please ensure your insulation contractor is aware of the vermiculite insulation &amp; follows proper Health Canada safety protocol. If there are any projects to complete in the attic (wiring for a new light or ceiling fan), these should be completed before more insulation is added, and again following vermiculite safety protocol. Once all work in the attic is done, and new insulation has been added, the attic hatch can be sealed with a "removable caulking" such as Draft Attack. If you decide not to add any further attic insulation, it is a good idea to seal the attic hatch now with the removable caulking.



Savings: By increasing the attic insulation from R32 up to a minimum R50, you could potentially save $ 174 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 250 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



If you decide to proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) eligible wall insulation upgrade, the rebate above would be doubled up to $ 500  from the DHERP.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

The windows on the main floor of your house have already been upgraded to newer triple pane units with two coatings of Low E and insulating gas filled spacers. There are still 3 older wood windows in the basement, that you were curious about the energy efficiency savings if you upgraded them to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend a full frame replacement window that is a triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By upgrading these 3 basement windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 31 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.0 GJ.



There are currently no rebates for window upgrades, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), where a $250 per eligible window upgrade is available - up to 12 windows. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

As outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report, your house is reasonably air tight and is expected to become tighter in conjunction with your planned renovations. If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



Almost all houses from the late 60's &amp; 70's would not have had an HRV and instead would manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by using exhaust fans (kitchen, bathroom, clothes dryer) and incorporate a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the heating system ductwork - that would allow outside air each time the boiler &amp; air handler operates or when an exhaust fan is operating.



As you "Renovate Tight", you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right", which means you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) that is a minimum 75% efficient in transferring heat from the outgoing air to the incoming fresh air. The furnace can still be used to circulate the incoming "pre-heated" air throughout the house.



I'm not sure how easily an HRV could be retrofitted into your house, so you will need to rely on your local heating &amp; ventilation contractor to design &amp; install a ventilation system that keeps your home healthy &amp; comfortable. It may just be they will indicate a system, such as the one I outlined in the 2nd paragraph above, will be the best solution. Once a new direct, vent condensing boiler is installed, you could even have them use the existing 4" combustion air supply hole to be used as a fresh air supply directly connected to the heating ductwork.



Please look at my Ventilation section in the "Additional Comments" of this report.



Savings: As your house is currently deemed to be "underventilated", adding more ventilation will increase heating cost. Installing a 75% efficient HRV is estimated to increase energy costs by about $ 200 per year.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

As your domestic hot water is provided in combination with your heating boiler, please see my upgrade recommendations in the previous section.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a 1980's Weil McLain oil boiler (estimated 82% efficiency) that feeds a fancoil to distribute heat throughout the house using the ductwork from a previous oil furnace that was in this house. The boiler is also used to heat the domestic hot water for this house year round - there was a hot water storage tank below the boiler, however it has been decommissioned and you are now using an electric hot water tank as storage (not connected to electricity).



There is ALOT of equipment in your mechanical room and you could significantly increase the efficiency &amp; reduce costs &amp; GHG's by looking at a new high-efficiency condensing gas "combination" boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE - either one with an internal 16 gallon domestic hot water storage or just use the current hot water tank for storage.



As you already have a fancoil to distribute the boiler heat through the ductwork, it may be better for you to keep the boiler setup, rather than converting back to a furnace again. A furnace can only really serve the thermostat on the upper floor, whereas having the boiler can let you run another thermostatically controlled zone specifically for the basement.



You do have the new pellet stove for the basement, so in a way you have the basement supplemental heat looked after.



Savings: By replacing your current oil combination boiler with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas combination boiler, you could potentially save $ 1913 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 46.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



If you decide to proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) eligible wall insulation upgrade, the rebate above would be doubled up to $ 2,500  from the DHERP.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The DHERP program may not be the best path forward for you, however I will still summarize the potential benefits below as you research this further. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,900 for the wall insulation

     - $   750 for 3 basement windows

     - $   500 for the ceiling insulation

     - $2,500 for the heating system

     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate if you exceed a 30% reduction it total energy use



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system rebate and the ceiling insulation, however these rebates would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:



     - ECM water circulation pump (boiler or domestic water circ pumps) - 50% up to $175 each (up to 5 per house)

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan, clothes dryer and the central vacuum), indicated a pressure drop of 6.5 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler vents up a chimney (ie: not "direct vent", so it is susceptible to back drafting).



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The main bathroom fan

        - The clothes dryer

        - The central vacuum cleaner



Overall, your house is relatively air tight and will become tighter after you complete a heating system upgrade (that eliminates the current chimney).



One of the biggest challenges for new home builders and existing home owners is to balance air tightness with adequate ventilation. Each house &amp; the occupants require a certain amount of fresh air in order to maintain a good indoor air quality, without over-ventilating &amp; wasting heat.



If your house was being built new today, it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit installed. These are harder to retrofit into older houses, so a more common way to add ventilation is to have an outside fresh air supply pipe directly connected to the return air plenum of your heating ductwork, and include a "manual damper" that you can adjust to regulate the amount of fresh air the house receives each time:

     - the boiler &amp; air handler operates

     - an exhaust fan (bathroom fan or dryer) operates



Some houses will add a timer switch to the main bathroom exhaust fan so that it will operate more frequently and draw in some outside fresh air each time it does.



You will know if your house is too tight in fall &amp; winter if there is excessive condensation building up on the windows or if odours (cooking or other) linger for too long &amp; the house starts to smell a bit "stale". If so, operate the exhaust fan &amp; open the fresh air supply damper a bit more.



You should consider upgrading to an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fan that use little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



